    "result_text": "There's exactly one test class in the project, and it passes. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the direct level rule with a points system and returned both `membershipPoints` and `membershipLevel`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`MembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 rewrote the helper. New `points(Owner)` computes: start at 0, +2 email present, +1 `namesakeCount == 0`, +2 household of 3+, +3 tenure over 365 days. `of(Owner)` now maps points \u2192 level: 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `membershipPoints` mapping (`MembershipLevel.points(owner)`) alongside the existing `membershipLevel` mapping, so both fields are returned.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` schema property and updated `membershipLevel`'s description/bounds (now 1\u20134, derived from points).\n\n4. **`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 this had a second, inline copy of the *old* level rule (start at 1, +1 email, +1 namesake, cap 3). Since the task replaces that rule, I pointed it at the shared `MembershipLevel.of(owner)` so the audit log stays consistent with the new system rather than logging a stale value.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreReturnsPointsAndLevel`, expecting `membershipPoints=3`, `membershipLevel=2` for a fresh owner with email + no namesake) passes, and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
